The RTT estimate as quic-go makes it, for a client, and a Retry as quic-go takes it.

quic-go's estimator (UpdateRTT, vendor/quic-go/internal/utils/rtt_stats.go:80-116) and upstream
ngtcp2's (ngtcp2_conn_update_rtt) agree on the formula and differ on the rest:

  - quic-go keeps latestRTT after the ACK Delay is taken off, ngtcp2 kept it before — and loss
    detection waits 9/8 of the larger of latest and smoothed;
  - quic-go caps the delay at max_ack_delay on every 1-RTT ACK
    (vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:436-439) and never drops a sample
    for it; ngtcp2 capped it only once the handshake was confirmed and until then dropped a sample
    the delay would take below min_rtt;
  - quic-go smooths in whole microseconds through float32, ngtcp2 in nanoseconds: after samples of
    20, 22 and 21 ms quic-go's smoothed RTT is 20.343 ms, ngtcp2's was 20.34375.

A client now runs conn_update_rtt_qgo; the server keeps upstream's estimator.

A Retry: quic-go keeps its congestion controller, clears the bytes in flight, the probe count and
the loss timer, and takes the Retry for an RTT sample of at least minRTTAfterRetry, 5 ms, when no
Initial was probed again (ResetForRetry,
vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:1153-1198). Upstream reset the controller
and took no sample; for this client the reset put a connection that runs quic-go's NewReno until
its authentication (patch 0018) on hysteria's BBR at once. A hysteria server does not send Retry,
so this was never seen — which is why it is written down.

Checked against quic-go's own sentPacketHandler and RTTStats, run by tools/qgovec: test/ngpatch.

